===Noun===
[[File:Canada_goose_flight_cropped_and_NR.jpg|thumb|right|Canadian goose]]
{{en-noun|~|geese}}

# Any of various grazing [[waterfowl]] of the family [[Anatidae]], which have feathers and webbed feet and are capable of flying, swimming, and walking on land, and which are bigger than [[duck]]s.
#: ''There is a flock of '''geese''' on the pond.''
# {{lb|en|strict}} A [[female]] goose.
# The flesh of the goose used as food.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1843|author=Charles Dickens|title=A Christmas Carol|chapter=[[s:A Christmas Carol (Dickens, 1843)/Stave 3|Stave 3: The Second of the Three Spirits]]|passage=Mrs. Cratchit made the gravy (ready beforehand in a little saucepan) hissing hot; Master Peter mashed the potatoes with incredible vigour; Miss Belinda sweetened up the apple-sauce; Martha dusted the hot plates; Bob took Tiny Tim beside him in a tiny corner at the table; the two young Cratchits set chairs for everybody, not forgetting themselves, and mounting guard upon their posts, crammed spoons into their mouths, lest they should shriek for '''goose''' before their turn came to be helped.}}
# {{lb|en|slang|plural {{m|en|geese}} or {{m|en|gooses}}}} A silly person.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1906|author=Langdon Mitchell|chapter=The New York Idea|title=Best Plays of the Early American Theatre, 1787-1911|page=430|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=xVSJZKaX3ScC|editor=John Gassner|isbn=0486410986|year_published=2000
|passage=I'm sorry for you, but you're such a '''goose'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Barbara Benedict|title=Love and Honor|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Jove Books|year=1994|page=65|isbn=0-515-11312-3|passage=Have you stopped to think, you '''gooses''', that Andy might not wish you to give it away?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Julie Berry (author)|Julie Berry]]|title=The Scandalous Sisterhood of Prickwillow Place|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Roaring Brook Press, [[w:Holtzbrinck Publishing Group|Holtzbrinck Publishing Holdings Limited Partnership]]|year=2014|isbn=978-1-59643-957-3|passage=You '''gooses'''. ''I'' didn’t accept his proposal. Mrs Plackett did. She did because she would. Don’t you see?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Julia London|title=The Princess Plan|publisher=[[w:Harlequin Enterprises|HQN Books]]|year=2019|isbn=978-1-474-09902-8|passage=Surely I needn’t explain to you '''gooses''' that ''none'' of you, not even ''you'', Caro, have the sort of dowry or connections or the ''appeal'' that such a match would require.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} A tailor's [[iron]], heated in live coals or embers, used to press fabrics.
#: {{syn|en|goose iron}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Macbeth|act=II|scene=iii|passage=Come in, tailor. Here you may roast your '''goose'''.}}
# {{lb|en|South Africa|slang|dated}} A [[young]] [[woman]] or [[girlfriend]].
# {{lb|en|uncountable|historical}} An old English [[board game]] in which players moved counters along a board, earning a double move when they reached the picture of a goose.

