#* {{quote-book|en|year=1933|author=w:Nathanael West|title=Miss Lonelyhearts|passage=She greeted Miss Lonelyhearts, then took hold of her husband and shook the breath out of him. When he was quiet, she dragged him into their apartment. Miss Lonelyhearts followed and as he passed her in the dark foyer, she '''goosed''' him and laughed.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:J P Donleavy|title=[[w:A_Singular_Man|A Singular Man]]|year=1963|year_published=1963 (USA)| page=36|passage=The witness stand. Goldminers giving evidence, sure he's violent didn't I see him with my own peepers chasing those poor kids up on the roof and he '''goosed''' my wife last Christmas. Violently. Just a forceful nudge of the knee.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[stimulate]]; to [[spur]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=December 7, 2021|author=Jesse Hassenger|title={{w|Leonardo DiCaprio}} and {{w|Jennifer Lawrence}} cope with disaster in the despairing satire ''{{w|Don't Look Up (2021 film)|Don’t Look Up}}''|url=https://www.avclub.com/leonardo-dicaprio-and-jennifer-lawrence-cope-with-disas-1848171832|work=AV Club|text=Almost everyone in McKay’s impossibly starry cast feels like they’re jumping into the ''{{w|Saturday Night Live|SNL}}'' host role, game for some light comedic lifting while waiting for the pros to show up and '''goose''' the laughs.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2023-07-10|author=w:James Poniewozik|title=The Twitter Watch Party Is Over|journal=w:The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/07/10/arts/television/tv-twitter-end-of-an-era.html|passage=The ensuing snarknado also seemed to '''goose''' the TV ratings. Hundreds of thousands of viewers switched on the movie after it began, suggesting that they’d gotten wind through Twitter of the bananas spectacle that was unfolding.}}
